      "head_commit": {
        "id": "a3ffa0b573a7b06a94c36e6489e1e753e860c040",
        "tree_id": "4d9a2d2b1dab16ed81736d97f9692b00f428c098",
        "message": "fix(69): two iOS targets shared a build directory, and the second deleted the first\n\nRun 36688154606. The device build was perfect:\n\n    libllama.a      5613800 bytes  Non-fat file: build-arm64/src/libllama.a\n                                       is architecture: arm64\n    libggml.a         39608 bytes  ... architecture: arm64\n    libggml-cpu.a   1039392 bytes  ... architecture: arm64\n    libggml-base.a  1002928 bytes  ... architecture: arm64\n    6 archives; llama, ggml, ggml-cpu and ggml-base all present and arm64\n\nand then the step reported a directory that does not exist:\n\n    === build-iossim ===\n    find: build-iossim: No such file or directory\n\nCAUSE: THE BUILD LOOP NAMED DIRECTORIES AFTER THE ARCHITECTURE, AND TWO OF THE\nTHREE TARGETS SHARE AN ARCHITECTURE.\n\n    while read -r ARCH SYSROOT; do\n      rm -rf \"build-$ARCH\"\n      cmake -S src-llama -B \"build-$ARCH\" ...\n\n        arm64  iphoneos              -> build-arm64\n        arm64  iphonesimulator       -> build-arm64   <- SAME NAME\n        x86_64 iphonesimulator       -> build-x86_64\n\nso the second iteration's `rm -rf build-arm64` deleted the DEVICE build, and the\nverify step then looked for `build-iossim` -- a name the loop never produced at\nall. Two names out of three were wrong.\n\nTHIS IS NOT COSMETIC, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH WRITING DOWN. iphoneos and\niphonesimulator are not interchangeable even at the SAME architecture. A device\nslice linked into a simulator build produces no compile error and no link error:\nit is a LOAD FAILURE at run time, on a device that will never boot. So a\ndirectory keyed on anything less than the full target triple risks shipping the\nplatform's object code under the other platform's name, which is exactly the\nclass of bug this repository keeps re-learning in a new costume:\n\n  - \"llamacpp-x86_64\" hardcoded in the artifact selector while its neighbours\n    were parameterised, so every ABI downloaded x86-64 archives\n  - \"i386\" compared against \"i686\" so every correct x86 build failed\n  - armv7a-linux-androideabi used as a sysroot DIRECTORY when the directory is\n    arm-linux-androideabi\n\nALL THREE NOW KEY ON THE FULL TRIPLE, and the loop, the verify step and the\npackaging step agree on the names:\n\n    build-aarch64-apple-ios      arm64  iphoneos\n    build-aarch64-apple-ios-sim  arm64  iphonesimulator\n    build-x86_64-apple-ios       x86_64 iphonesimulator\n\nextracted and run with echo in place of cmake, all three appear and none collides.\nThe printed table above the loop now names the directory as well as the\narchitecture, because a reader comparing it against the verify step should not\nhave to work out the mapping.\n\nThe device build in that run is also the first evidence that llama.cpp\ncross-compiles for iOS at all: 6 archives, all arm64, at the pinned commit.\nMSG",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-30T08:21:17Z",
